      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Brightwell Foods, a fictional company. You're covering the finance office's phone and inbox on Friday afternoon. Three voicemails and two emails are waiting. Some are real and some were made to trick you, and you can't always tell by the voice. Sort them, and verify or report anything that doesn't sit right. Ringing back when you're unsure is always fine and never marked down, though a real caller does take a colleague's time to check.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "Nothing in it tripped a check this time. Next, the same kind of message reaches the finance office, and you're the one reading it.",
        "noBlameLine": "A familiar voice is hard to doubt, and these calls were built to lean on exactly that trust, so anyone can be caught by one.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: a voice you recognise is not proof of who is calling, and neither is an email that backs it up. If a call or voicemail asks for money, secrecy or a skipped step, ring the person back on a number you already hold, then report it using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "A familiar voice feels like proof, and these messages were made to use that, so anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away and why ringing back on a number you already hold works whoever is speaking.",
        "defendIntro": "Go through the voicemails and emails and decide what to do with each one. Read each voicemail's transcript: what it asks you to do matters more than whose voice it is. Work through them at your own pace; it all comes back to ringing the person yourself.",
        "voicePanel": {
          "heading": "How a voice gets copied",
          "text": "Recordings of someone speaking in public, such as a talk, an interview or a company video, can be used to make new sentences in a voice that sounds like theirs. If you can't reach the person to check, ask someone who would know, such as the finance manager."
        }
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      }
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
      }
    }
